module IO/File

import IO/Errors
import IO/Stream

# Everything that touches the disk goes through a file manager: making, reading, writing, opening, mapping, deleting,
# renaming, moving, copying, asking about and listing files and directories. A `File` or a `Directory` only says
# where, so building one touches nothing.
#
# Storage comes in three layers. A {StorageManager} is one storage device (a disk, an SD card) and its partition
# table. A {PartitionManager} is one partition of it. The file system on one partition keeps its file table, gives files
# their space and does every read and write. A partition has exactly one file manager. The operating system plays all
# three, and {FileManager} is the file manager a program calls: it reaches every partition the OS has mounted, one OS
# file manager per partition.

# ============================================================================
# File — named, persistent, format-blind byte content
# ============================================================================

### A `File` names persistent byte content in the filesystem — the data-at-rest itself, NOT an open
### handle. It is a cheap value (just the path), constructing one opens nothing. {FileManager} opens one of
### three sessions over its content:
###   * `FileManager.open_storage[T](at:)` — a random-access {FileStorage} of fixed-size cells, no cursor,
###   * `FileManager.open_read(at:)` — a sequential {Source} (consume forward with `load`),
###   * `FileManager.open_write(at:)` / `open_append(at:)` — a sequential {Sink} (emit forward with `store`).
### The content outlives every session (closing a session leaves the file intact — criterion: a file
### persists beyond the program). Things that are NOT files — a socket, pipe, or console — have no such
### content layer: they are born as {Source}/{Sink} with nothing addressable underneath, so they can
### never be opened as storage.
record File
    ### The path naming this file's content.
    at: Text

# To PRODUCE output through a storage (a mapping cannot grow a file), make and size the file first, then open it:
#   var f = FileManager.make_file(at: File(at: out), replacing: true)
#   FileManager.set_size(at: f, bytes: count)
#   var storage = FileManager.open_storage[Byte](at: f)
#   modifying storage as s
#       ...
# For a file larger than the address space, open one window at a time with `open_storage_range[T](at:, start:,
# count:)`.

# ============================================================================
# FileStorage[T] — a file seen as fixed-size cells of T, random access
# ============================================================================

### A file seen as fixed-size cells of `T`, read and written in place by index, with no cursor: what the OS's file
### mapping gives (CreateFileMapping / MapViewOfFile, mmap). `FileStorage[Byte]` is one byte per cell.
### `FileStorage[Char]` is one Unicode scalar per cell, four bytes each, UTF-32LE on disk (a UTF-8 text file is not
### one: read it as `Text`, or as `FileStorage[Byte]`). Zero-copy: the cells are not read into memory up front but
### brought in on access.
###
### A cell is read with `storage[i]` and written with `storage[i] = value`: through a `viewing storage as s` block
### (or any read-only token) for reading and a `modifying storage as s` block for writing. A storage is opened for writing when the file can be written, otherwise for
### reading only, and writing a cell of one opened for reading only fails with {NotEnoughPermissionError}. A
### storage cannot grow its file: size the file first with `FileManager.set_size`. Writes land in the file,
### flushed lazily by the OS, on `flush()`, and when the storage ends at the end of its scope.
entity FileStorage[T]
needs T obeys StorageCell
    ### The first cell asked for (non-owning, released when the storage ends).
    secret buf: Hijacked[T]
    ### The size of the cells in bytes, the cell count is `byte_len // T.data_size()`.
    secret byte_len: U64
    ### Bytes from the real mapping base to `buf`: nonzero only for a range whose start was rounded down to the
    ### mapping granularity. Releasing the storage strides back by this to unmap the true base.
    secret map_delta: U64
    ### Whether the file was opened for writing.
    secret writable: Bool
    ### Whether the mapping has been released. Guards `close()` so it runs once.
    secret is_closed: Bool

# ============================================================================
# FileSource — a File-backed sequential read stream (obeys Source)
# ============================================================================

### A sequential read session over a {File}'s content. It holds one OS descriptor with a `close`
### teardown, so it is an `entity` (never copied, so the descriptor is never closed twice). Consume
### it forward with `load`, there is no length, index, or seek — the file's spatial extent lives on
### {FileStorage}, not here.
entity FileSource
obeys Source, Enterable
    ### The native file descriptor.
    secret fd: S32
    ### Whether the descriptor has been closed. Guards `close()` so it is idempotent.
    secret is_closed: Bool

# ============================================================================
# FileSink — a File-backed sequential write stream (obeys Sink)
# ============================================================================

### A sequential write session over a {File}'s content (truncating via `open_write`, or appending via
### `open_append`). It holds one OS descriptor with a `close` teardown, so it is an `entity`. Emit into
### it forward with `store`, there is no length, index, or seek.
entity FileSink
obeys Sink, Enterable
    ### The native file descriptor.
    secret fd: S32
    ### Whether the descriptor has been closed. Guards `close()` so it is idempotent.
    secret is_closed: Bool

# ============================================================================
# Directory — a named location in the filesystem that contains entries
# ============================================================================

### A `Directory` names a directory in the filesystem — a container of entries, NOT byte content (that
### is what {File} is). Like `File` it is a cheap value (just the path), constructing one creates
### nothing. It builds the paths of the things inside it (`file(name:)` → a {File}, `go_to(name:)` → a
### nested `Directory`). {FileManager} makes, lists and removes it.
record Directory
    ### The path naming this directory.
    at: Text

### One directory entry: its bare name, kind, and last-modification time.
record DirEntry
    name: Text  # The final path component
    is_directory: Bool  # Whether the entry is a directory
    modified: Moment  # Last-modification time (from the directory read)

### Filesystem metadata for a path, captured in a single stat.
record FileMetadata
    size: S64  # Size in bytes
    modified: Moment  # Last-modification time
    created: Moment  # Creation time (inode-change time on POSIX, best-effort)
    is_directory: Bool  # Whether the path is a directory
    readonly: Bool  # Whether the path is marked readonly

# ============================================================================
# The storage layers
# ============================================================================

### One storage device (a disk, an SD card), as the operating system reports it: its blocks and its partition table.
### The OS manages it, and this is what the program can ask about it.
record StorageManager
    ### The OS's name for the device (`/dev/sda`, `\\.\PhysicalDrive0`).
    name: Text
    ### Its size in bytes, 0 when the OS doesn't say.
    size: U64

### One partition, a slice of a storage device, as the operating system reports it. A partition with a file system
### has exactly one file manager, the OS's, reached through {FileManager}.
record PartitionManager
    ### The OS's name for the partition (`/dev/sda1`, `C:\`).
    name: Text
    ### The name of the {StorageManager} it is on, empty when the OS doesn't say.
    storage: Text
    ### Where its files are reached (a mount point, a drive's root).
    root: Directory
    ### The file system on it (`ext4`, `NTFS`, `apfs`).
    file_system: Text
    ### Its size in bytes, 0 when the OS doesn't say.
    size: U64

### The operating system's file manager: the one place a program does file system work. Its routines are
### called on the type (`FileManager.read_text(at:)`), and every one that touches the disk is here. The OS keeps
### one file manager per mounted partition, and `FileManager` reaches all of them: `partition(at:)` tells which
### partition a path is on.
record FileManager
    pass

# ============================================================================
# Paths — pure, no OS calls
# ============================================================================

### A runtime byte buffer of `len` bytes as text.
### :param ptr: The buffer.
### :param len: Its length.
### :returns: The text.
secret routine path_text(ptr: Hijacked[Byte], len: U64) -> Text
    return Bytes(ptr: ptr, count: len).decode_as_utf8_lossy()

### Two path segments joined with the platform separator.
### :param a: The left segment.
### :param b: The right segment.
### :returns: The joined path.
secret routine join_path(a: Text, b: Text) -> Text
    var a_cstr = a.to[CStr]()
    var b_cstr = b.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_join_path(a_cstr, a_cstr.count().to[S32](), b_cstr, b_cstr.count().to[S32]())
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())

### The parent of a path, empty when it has none.
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The parent path.
secret routine parent_path(path: Text) -> Text
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_parent_path(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())

### The final component of a path.
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The final component.
secret routine last_component(path: Text) -> Text
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_file_name(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())

### The final component of a path without its extension.
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The stem.
secret routine path_stem(path: Text) -> Text
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_file_stem(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())

### The extension of a path, without the leading dot, empty when it has none.
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The extension.
secret routine path_extension(path: Text) -> Text
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_extension(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())

### This file's extension, without the leading dot (empty when absent).
### :returns: The extension `Text`.
@readonly
routine File.extension() -> Text
    return path_extension(path: me.at)

### This file's final path component.
### :returns: The name `Text`.
@readonly
routine File.name() -> Text
    return last_component(path: me.at)

### This file's final component without its extension.
### :returns: The stem `Text`.
@readonly
routine File.stem() -> Text
    return path_stem(path: me.at)

### The directory this file is in.
### :returns: The parent `Directory`.
@readonly
routine File.parent() -> Directory
    return Directory(at: parent_path(path: me.at))

### This file with its extension replaced (an empty `ext` drops the extension).
### :param ext: The new extension WITHOUT a leading dot.
### :returns: A new `File` naming the rebuilt path.
@readonly
routine File.with_extension(ext: Text) -> File
    var stem = path_stem(path: me.at)
    var base = if ext.is_empty() then stem else f"{stem}.{ext}"
    var dir = parent_path(path: me.at)
    return File(at: if dir.is_empty() then base else join_path(a: dir, b: base))

### This file with its final component replaced, in the same directory.
### :param name: The new final component.
### :returns: A new `File` naming the rebuilt path.
@readonly
routine File.with_name(name: Text) -> File
    var dir = parent_path(path: me.at)
    return File(at: if dir.is_empty() then name else join_path(a: dir, b: name))

### This directory's final path component.
### :returns: The name `Text`.
@readonly
routine Directory.name() -> Text
    return last_component(path: me.at)

### The directory this directory is in.
### :returns: The parent `Directory`.
@readonly
routine Directory.parent() -> Directory
    return Directory(at: parent_path(path: me.at))

### The {File} named `name` directly inside this directory.
### :param name: The child file's final component.
### :returns: A `File` at `<this>/<name>`.
@readonly
routine Directory.file(name: Text) -> File
    return File(at: join_path(a: me.at, b: name))

### The directory named `name` directly inside this directory: going to it by name, as a path. Nothing on disk is
### touched.
### :param name: The child directory's final component.
### :returns: A `Directory` at `<this>/<name>`.
@readonly
routine Directory.go_to(name: Text) -> Directory
    return Directory(at: join_path(a: me.at, b: name))

# ============================================================================
# Why a file system call failed
# ============================================================================

### Throws the crashable for why the last file system call on this thread failed: {NotFoundError},
### {NotEnoughPermissionError}, {NotEnoughSpaceError}, {StorageFaultError}, or {IOError} for any other reason.
### :param action: What was being done, as the start of the message (`Cannot read 'notes.txt'`).
### :param path: The path the call worked on.
### :throws: Always, the crashable for the reason.
secret failable routine fail_io(action: Text, path: Text)
    var reason = C::rf_fs_last_error()
    if reason == 2
        throw NotFoundError(description: f"{action}: nothing is there")
    if reason == 3
        throw NotEnoughPermissionError(description: f"{action}: this program is not allowed to")
    if reason == 4
        throw NotEnoughSpaceError(description: f"{action}: the storage device is full")
    if reason == 5
        throw StorageFaultError(description: f"{action}: this storage device failed to read or write at '{path}'")
    throw IOError(description: action)

# ============================================================================
# The storage layers — asking the OS
# ============================================================================

### A {PartitionManager} from one line of the runtime's partition listing: name, device, root, file system and size,
### separated by tabs.
### :param line: The line.
### :returns: The partition.
secret routine partition_of_line(line: Text) -> PartitionManager
    var fields = line.split(separator: '\t')
    while fields.count() < 5
        fields.add_last(value: "")
    return PartitionManager(
        name: fields[0],
        storage: fields[1],
        root: Directory(at: fields[2]),
        file_system: fields[3],
        size: try fields[4].to[U64]() ?? 0,
    )

### The lines of a runtime listing, none for an empty or a failed one.
### :param ptr: The listing.
### :param len: Its length.
### :returns: The lines.
secret routine listing_lines(ptr: Hijacked[Byte], len: U64) -> List[Text]
    if ptr.is_none() or len == 0
        return List[Text]()
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: len).split(separator: '\n')

### The storage devices the operating system reports, none where it reports none.
### :returns: The devices.
common routine StorageManager.all() -> List[StorageManager]
    var ptr = C::rf_storage_list()
    var result = List[StorageManager]()
    each line in listing_lines(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())
        var fields = line.split(separator: '\t')
        if fields.count() >= 2
            result.add_last(value: StorageManager(name: fields[0], size: try fields[1].to[U64]() ?? 0))
    return result

### The partitions on this device the operating system reports.
### :returns: The partitions.
@readonly
routine StorageManager.partitions() -> List[PartitionManager]
    var result = List[PartitionManager]()
    each partition in PartitionManager.all()
        if partition.storage == me.name
            result.add_last(value: partition)
    return result

### The partitions the operating system has mounted, none where it reports none.
### :returns: The partitions.
common routine PartitionManager.all() -> List[PartitionManager]
    var ptr = C::rf_partition_list()
    var result = List[PartitionManager]()
    each line in listing_lines(ptr: ptr, len: C::rf_get_result_len())
        result.add_last(value: partition_of_line(line: line))
    return result

### The partition the text `path` is on.
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The partition.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is at the path, {IOError} when the OS can't tell.
secret failable routine partition_at(path: Text) -> PartitionManager
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_partition_of(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    var len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    if ptr.is_none() or len == 0
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot tell the partition of '{path}'", path: path)
    return partition_of_line(line: path_text(ptr: ptr, len: len))

### The partition the directory is on: its file manager is the OS's file manager for that partition.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The partition.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is at the path, {IOError} when the OS can't tell.
failable common routine FileManager.partition(at: Directory) -> PartitionManager
    return partition_at(path: at.at)

### The partition the file is on: its file manager is the OS's file manager for that partition.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The partition.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is at the path, {IOError} when the OS can't tell.
failable common routine FileManager.partition(at: File) -> PartitionManager
    return partition_at(path: at.at)

# ============================================================================
# FileManager — asking about a path
# ============================================================================

### Whether something exists at this file's path.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: `true` when the path exists.
common routine FileManager.exists(at: File) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_exists(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether something exists at this directory's path.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: `true` when the path exists.
common routine FileManager.exists(at: Directory) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_exists(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether the path names a regular file. A missing path gives `false`.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: `true` when the path is a regular file.
common routine FileManager.is_file(at: File) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_is_file(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether the path names a directory. A missing path gives `false`.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: `true` when the path is a directory.
common routine FileManager.is_directory(at: Directory) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_is_directory(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### The size of a file in bytes.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The size in bytes.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the file cannot be inspected.
failable common routine FileManager.size(at: File) -> S64
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var size = C::rf_fs_file_size(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    if size == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot read the size of '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return size

### The metadata of the path, captured in a single stat.
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The resulting {FileMetadata}.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the path cannot be inspected.
secret failable routine metadata_of(path: Text) -> FileMetadata
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var status = C::rf_fs_metadata(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    if status == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot read the metadata of '{path}'", path: path)
    return FileMetadata(
        size: C::rf_fs_meta_size(),
        modified: Moment.from_timestamp(seconds: C::rf_fs_meta_modified()),
        created: Moment.from_timestamp(seconds: C::rf_fs_meta_created()),
        is_directory: C::rf_fs_meta_is_directory(),
        readonly: C::rf_fs_meta_readonly(),
    )

### A file's metadata, captured in a single stat.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The resulting {FileMetadata}.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the file cannot be inspected.
failable common routine FileManager.metadata(at: File) -> FileMetadata
    return metadata_of(path: at.at)

### A directory's metadata, captured in a single stat.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The resulting {FileMetadata}.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the directory cannot be inspected.
failable common routine FileManager.metadata(at: Directory) -> FileMetadata
    return metadata_of(path: at.at)

### Whether this process can read the path.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
common routine FileManager.can_read(at: File) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_can_read(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether this process can read the directory.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
common routine FileManager.can_read(at: Directory) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_can_read(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether this process can write to the path.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
common routine FileManager.can_write(at: File) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_can_write(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether this process can write to the directory.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
common routine FileManager.can_write(at: Directory) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_can_write(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether this process can execute the file.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
common routine FileManager.can_execute(at: File) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_can_execute(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### Whether this process can enter the directory.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
common routine FileManager.can_execute(at: Directory) -> Bool
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    return C::rf_fs_can_execute(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())

### The absolute path of the text `path` (symlinks and other non-canonical components kept).
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The absolute path.
### :throws: {IOError} when the path cannot be resolved.
secret failable routine absolute_path(path: Text) -> Text
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_absolute_path(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    var len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    if ptr.is_none()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot resolve the absolute path of '{path}'", path: path)
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: len)

### The canonical path of the text `path` (casing normalized, links and relative segments resolved).
### :param path: The path.
### :returns: The canonical path.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the path cannot be resolved.
secret failable routine canonical_path(path: Text) -> Text
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_canonical_path(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    var len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    if ptr.is_none()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot resolve the canonical path of '{path}'", path: path)
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: len)

### The file at its absolute path (symlinks and other non-canonical components kept).
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: A new `File` naming the absolute path.
### :throws: {IOError} when the path cannot be resolved.
failable common routine FileManager.absolute(at: File) -> File
    return File(at: absolute_path(path: at.at))

### The directory at its absolute path (symlinks and other non-canonical components kept).
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: A new `Directory` naming the absolute path.
### :throws: {IOError} when the path cannot be resolved.
failable common routine FileManager.absolute(at: Directory) -> Directory
    return Directory(at: absolute_path(path: at.at))

### The file at its canonical location (casing normalized, links and relative segments resolved).
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: A new `File` naming the canonical path.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the path cannot be resolved.
failable common routine FileManager.canonical(at: File) -> File
    return File(at: canonical_path(path: at.at))

### The directory at its canonical location (casing normalized, links and relative segments resolved).
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: A new `Directory` naming the canonical path.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the path cannot be resolved.
failable common routine FileManager.canonical(at: Directory) -> Directory
    return Directory(at: canonical_path(path: at.at))

# ============================================================================
# FileManager — making, removing, renaming, moving, copying
# ============================================================================

### Makes the file. Its directory must exist. An existing file is an error, unless `replacing` is true: then it
### is emptied.
### :param at: The file to make.
### :param replacing: Whether an existing file is emptied instead of being an error.
### :returns: The file, made.
### :throws: {IOError} when it already exists (and `replacing` is false), {NotFoundError} when its directory is
###     missing, {NotEnoughPermissionError}, {NotEnoughSpaceError} or {StorageFaultError} for those reasons.
failable common routine FileManager.make_file(at: File, replacing: Bool = false) -> File
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var result = C::rf_fs_make_file(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32](), replacing: replacing)
    if result == 1
        throw IOError(description: f"Cannot make '{at.at}': it already exists. Pass 'replacing: true' to empty it")
    if result == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot make '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return at

### Makes the directory. An existing directory is an error. With `parents`, missing directories above it are made
### too, and one that already exists is kept.
### :param at: The directory to make.
### :param parents: Whether missing directories above it are made too.
### :returns: The directory, made.
### :throws: {IOError} when the directory cannot be made, or the crashable for the reason.
failable common routine FileManager.make_directory(at: Directory, parents: Bool = false) -> Directory
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var result: S32 = 0
    if parents
        result = C::rf_fs_create_dir_all(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    else
        result = C::rf_fs_create_dir(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    if result == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot make the directory '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return at

### Removes the file.
### :param at: The file.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the file cannot be removed.
failable common routine FileManager.delete(at: File)
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    if C::rf_fs_remove(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]()) == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot remove '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return

### Removes the directory, which must be empty. `delete_all` removes one with contents.
### :param at: The directory.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the directory cannot be removed.
failable common routine FileManager.delete(at: Directory)
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    if C::rf_fs_remove(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]()) == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot remove the directory '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return

### Removes the directory and everything in it.
### :param at: The directory.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, {IOError} when the tree cannot be removed.
failable common routine FileManager.delete_all(at: Directory)
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    if C::rf_fs_remove_all(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]()) == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot remove the directory tree '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return

### Gives the path `path` the name `name`, in the same directory.
### :param path: The path.
### :param name: The new name.
### :param replacing: Whether something already called `name` there is replaced.
### :returns: The new path.
### :throws: {IOError} when the name holds a separator or is taken, or the crashable for the reason.
secret failable routine rename_path(path: Text, name: Text, replacing: Bool) -> Text
    var src = path.to[CStr]()
    var new_name = name.to[CStr]()
    var result = C::rf_fs_rename(
        path: src,
        path_len: src.count().to[S32](),
        name: new_name,
        name_len: new_name.count().to[S32](),
        replacing: replacing,
    )
    if result == 1
        throw IOError(description: f"Cannot rename '{path}' to '{name}': something there has that name. Pass 'replacing: true' to replace it")
    if result == 2
        throw IOError(description: f"Cannot rename '{path}' to '{name}': a name holds no separator, put it in another directory with 'move'")
    if result == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot rename '{path}' to '{name}'", path: path)
    var dir = parent_path(path: path)
    return if dir.is_empty() then name else join_path(a: dir, b: name)

### Changes the file's name, in the directory it is in. Only the name changes: `to` is a name, not a path, and one
### with a separator in it fails (put the file in another directory with `move`).
### :param at: The file.
### :param to: Its new name.
### :param replacing: Whether a file already called `to` there is replaced.
### :returns: The file under its new name.
### :throws: {IOError} when the name holds a separator or is taken (and `replacing` is false), {NotFoundError} when
###     nothing is at `at`, or the crashable for another reason.
@discardable
failable common routine FileManager.rename(at: File, to: Text, replacing: Bool = false) -> File
    return File(at: rename_path(path: at.at, name: to, replacing: replacing))

### Changes the directory's name, in the directory it is in. Only the name changes: `to` is a name, not a path, and
### one with a separator in it fails (put the directory in another one with `move`).
### :param at: The directory.
### :param to: Its new name.
### :param replacing: Whether an empty directory already called `to` there is replaced.
### :returns: The directory under its new name.
### :throws: {IOError} when the name holds a separator or is taken (and `replacing` is false), {NotFoundError} when
###     nothing is at `at`, or the crashable for another reason.
@discardable
failable common routine FileManager.rename(at: Directory, to: Text, replacing: Bool = false) -> Directory
    return Directory(at: rename_path(path: at.at, name: to, replacing: replacing))

### Puts the path `path` into the directory `into`, keeping its name: a copy into `into` and then a delete of the
### original. On the same partition the OS renames it instead, which gives the same result.
### :param path: The path.
### :param into: The directory it goes into.
### :param replacing: Whether something of the same name in `into` is replaced.
### :returns: The new path.
### :throws: {IOError} when something of that name is there (and `replacing` is false) or when the copy was made but
###     the original couldn't be deleted (both are there then), or the crashable for the reason.
secret failable routine move_path(path: Text, into: Text, replacing: Bool) -> Text
    var src = path.to[CStr]()
    var dir = into.to[CStr]()
    var result = C::rf_fs_move_into(
        path: src,
        path_len: src.count().to[S32](),
        into: dir,
        into_len: dir.count().to[S32](),
        replacing: replacing,
    )
    if result == 1
        throw IOError(description: f"Cannot move '{path}' into '{into}': something there has its name. Pass 'replacing: true' to replace it")
    if result == -2
        fail_io(action: f"Moved a copy of '{path}' into '{into}', but cannot delete the original, so both are there", path: path)
    if result == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot move '{path}' into '{into}'", path: path)
    return join_path(a: into, b: last_component(path: path))

### Puts the file into the directory `into`, keeping its name. A move is a copy into `into` and then a delete of the
### original, and it is not atomic: if the copy fails nothing is deleted, and if the delete fails both are there and
### the error says so. On the same partition the OS renames the file instead, which gives the same result.
### :param at: The file.
### :param into: The directory it goes into, which must exist.
### :param replacing: Whether a file of the same name in `into` is replaced.
### :returns: The file in its new directory.
### :throws: {IOError} when a file of that name is there (and `replacing` is false) or when the original couldn't be
###     deleted after the copy, {NotFoundError} when nothing is at `at` or `into` is missing, or the crashable for
###     another reason.
@discardable
failable common routine FileManager.move(at: File, into: Directory, replacing: Bool = false) -> File
    return File(at: move_path(path: at.at, into: into.at, replacing: replacing))

### Puts the directory, with everything in it, into the directory `into`, keeping its name. A move is a copy into
### `into` and then a delete of the original, and it is not atomic: if the copy fails nothing is deleted, and if the
### delete fails both are there and the error says so. On the same partition the OS renames the directory instead,
### which gives the same result.
### :param at: The directory.
### :param into: The directory it goes into, which must exist.
### :param replacing: Whether an empty directory of the same name in `into` is replaced.
### :returns: The directory in its new place.
### :throws: {IOError} when a directory of that name is there (and `replacing` is false) or when the original
###     couldn't be deleted after the copy, {NotFoundError} when nothing is at `at` or `into` is missing, or the
###     crashable for another reason.
@discardable
failable common routine FileManager.move(at: Directory, into: Directory, replacing: Bool = false) -> Directory
    return Directory(at: move_path(path: at.at, into: into.at, replacing: replacing))

### Copies the file. An existing destination is an error unless `replacing` is true.
### :param from: The file to copy.
### :param to: Where the copy goes.
### :param replacing: Whether an existing destination is replaced.
### :throws: {IOError} when the destination exists (and `replacing` is false), or the crashable for why the copy
###     failed.
failable common routine FileManager.copy(from: File, to: File, replacing: Bool = false)
    var src = from.at.to[CStr]()
    var dst = to.at.to[CStr]()
    var result: S32 = 0
    if replacing
        result = C::rf_fs_copy(src, src.count().to[S32](), dst, dst.count().to[S32]())
    else
        result = C::rf_fs_copy_no_replace(src, src.count().to[S32](), dst, dst.count().to[S32]())
    if result == 1
        throw IOError(description: f"Cannot copy '{from.at}' to '{to.at}': it already exists. Pass 'replacing: true' to replace it")
    if result == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot copy '{from.at}' to '{to.at}'", path: from.at)
    return

### Copies the directory and everything in it. Symbolic links are not followed. An existing destination is an
### error unless `replacing` is true: then the tree is copied into it, replacing files of the same names.
### :param from: The directory to copy.
### :param to: Where the copy goes.
### :param replacing: Whether an existing destination is copied into.
### :throws: {IOError} when the destination exists (and `replacing` is false), or the crashable for why an entry
###     could not be copied.
failable common routine FileManager.copy(from: Directory, to: Directory, replacing: Bool = false)
    if FileManager.exists(at: to)
        if not replacing
            throw IOError(description: f"Cannot copy '{from.at}' to '{to.at}': it already exists. Pass 'replacing: true' to copy into it")
    else
        drop FileManager.make_directory(at: to, parents: true)
    each entry in FileManager.entries(at: from)
        if entry.is_directory
            FileManager.copy(from: from.go_to(name: entry.name), to: to.go_to(name: entry.name), replacing: true)
        else
            FileManager.copy(from: from.file(name: entry.name), to: to.file(name: entry.name), replacing: true)
    return

### Sets the readonly attribute of the file (file system metadata).
### :param at: The file.
### :param readonly: `true` to make it readonly, `false` to clear it.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, or the crashable for why the attribute cannot be set.
failable common routine FileManager.set_readonly(at: File, readonly: Bool = true)
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    if C::rf_fs_set_readonly(cstr, cstr.count().to[S32](), readonly) == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot set readonly on '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return

### Sets the readonly attribute of the directory (file system metadata).
### :param at: The directory.
### :param readonly: `true` to make it readonly, `false` to clear it.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when nothing is there, or the crashable for why the attribute cannot be set.
failable common routine FileManager.set_readonly(at: Directory, readonly: Bool = true)
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    if C::rf_fs_set_readonly(cstr, cstr.count().to[S32](), readonly) == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot set readonly on '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return

### Sets an existing file's size to exactly `bytes` (extending with zero bytes or truncating). Size a file before
### opening it as storage to produce output through it.
### :param at: The file.
### :param bytes: The exact size in bytes.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why its size cannot be set.
failable common routine FileManager.set_size(at: File, bytes: U64)
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    if C::rf_fs_set_size(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32](), size: bytes) == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot set the size of '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return

# ============================================================================
# FileManager — whole-file reads and writes
# ============================================================================

### Reads a whole file as text (UTF-8, lossy). UNCOLORED, like `waitfor`: inside work an Agent runs as a fiber
### (`start_now()`/`start_here()`) the fiber PARKS while a worker thread does the blocking read (its siblings keep
### progressing), anywhere else it reads inline. This is the way to read a whole file from such work.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: Its contents as `Text`.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be read.
failable common routine FileManager.read_text(at: File) -> Text
    return FileManager.read_bytes(at: at).decode_as_utf8_lossy()

### Reads a whole file. UNCOLORED, like `read_text`.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: Its contents.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be read.
failable common routine FileManager.read_bytes(at: File) -> Bytes
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_io_read_file_all(cstr, cstr.count().to[S32]())
    if C::rf_io_last_failed()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot read '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    return Bytes(ptr: ptr, count: C::rf_io_get_result_len())

### Writes `content` to an existing file, replacing what it held. UNCOLORED, like `read_text`. The text is encoded as
### UTF-8.
### :param at: The file, which must exist (make it with `make_file`).
### :param content: The text to write.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be written.
failable common routine FileManager.write_text(at: File, content: Text)
    var data = content.to[CStr]()
    write_all(path: at.at, data: data.view().ptr(), count: data.count())
    return

### Writes `content` to an existing file, replacing what it held. UNCOLORED, like `read_text`.
### :param at: The file, which must exist (make it with `make_file`).
### :param content: The bytes to write.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be written.
failable common routine FileManager.write_bytes(at: File, content: Bytes)
    danger
        write_all(path: at.at, data: content.raw_buffer(), count: content.count())
    return

### Writes `count` bytes at `data` to the existing file at `path`, replacing what it held.
### :param path: The file's path.
### :param data: The bytes.
### :param count: How many.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be written.
secret failable routine write_all(path: Text, data: Hijacked[Byte], count: U64)
    var cstr = path.to[CStr]()
    drop C::rf_io_write_file_all(
        path: cstr,
        path_len: cstr.count().to[S32](),
        data: data,
        data_len: count.to[S64](),
    )
    if C::rf_io_last_failed()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot write '{path}'. Make it with 'FileManager.make_file' first", path: path)
    return

### Adds `content` at the end of an existing file. The text is encoded as UTF-8.
### :param at: The file, which must exist (make it with `make_file`).
### :param content: The text to add.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be written.
failable common routine FileManager.append_text(at: File, content: Text)
    using FileManager.open_append(at: at) as sink
        drop sink.store(data: content.encode_as_utf8())
    return

# ============================================================================
# FileManager — sessions over a file's content
# ============================================================================

### Opens the file for sequential reading, giving a {Source} consumed forward with `load`.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: A {FileSource} over the file's content, to be bound by `using`.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be opened for reading.
failable common routine FileManager.open_read(at: File) -> FileSource
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var fd = C::rf_file_open(cstr, cstr.count().to[S32](), 0)
    if fd == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot open '{at.at}' for reading", path: at.at)
    return FileSource(fd: fd, is_closed: false)

### Opens an existing file for sequential writing, EMPTYING it first, giving a {Sink} filled with `store`.
### :param at: The file, which must exist (make it with `make_file`).
### :returns: A {FileSink} over the file's content, to be bound by `using`.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be opened for writing.
failable common routine FileManager.open_write(at: File) -> FileSink
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var fd = C::rf_file_open(cstr, cstr.count().to[S32](), 1)
    if fd == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot open '{at.at}' for writing", path: at.at)
    return FileSink(fd: fd, is_closed: false)

### Opens an existing file for sequential appending (writes land after its contents), giving a {Sink}.
### :param at: The file, which must exist (make it with `make_file`).
### :returns: A {FileSink} over the file's content, to be bound by `using`.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be opened for appending.
failable common routine FileManager.open_append(at: File) -> FileSink
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var fd = C::rf_file_open(cstr, cstr.count().to[S32](), 2)
    if fd == -1
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot open '{at.at}' for appending", path: at.at)
    return FileSink(fd: fd, is_closed: false)

### Opens the whole file as a {FileStorage} of `T` cells (`Byte` or `Char`): for reading and writing when the file
### can be written, for reading only otherwise. An empty file gives a storage of no cells. Bytes past the last whole
### cell are not part of it.
### :param at: The file.
### :returns: The storage, ended at the end of its scope.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be opened.
failable common routine FileManager.open_storage[T](at: File) -> FileStorage[T]
needs T obeys StorageCell
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var buf = C::rf_file_map_storage(path: cstr, path_len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    if buf.is_none()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot open '{at.at}' as storage", path: at.at)
    var byte_len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    var esize = T.data_size().byte_size()
    return FileStorage[T](
        buf: buf.recast_as[T](),
        byte_len: byte_len - byte_len % esize,
        map_delta: 0,
        writable: C::rf_file_map_writable(),
        is_closed: false,
    )

### Opens a WINDOW of the file as a {FileStorage} of `T` cells: `count` cells starting at cell `start`, clamped to
### the file's end, for reading and writing when the file can be written. This is the way through a file larger
### than the address space. The window's own `count()` may be smaller than `count` when it reaches the end, and a
### `start` at or past the end gives a storage of no cells.
### :param at: The file.
### :param start: The first cell.
### :param count: The number of cells (clamped to the file's end).
### :returns: The storage, ended at the end of its scope.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the file doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it can't be opened.
failable common routine FileManager.open_storage_range[T](at: File, start: U64, count: U64) -> FileStorage[T]
needs T obeys StorageCell
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var esize = T.data_size().byte_size()
    var buf = C::rf_file_map_storage_range(
        path: cstr,
        path_len: cstr.count().to[S32](),
        offset: start *% esize,
        length: count *% esize,
    )
    if buf.is_none()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot open a range of '{at.at}' as storage", path: at.at)
    var user_len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    var delta = C::rf_file_map_delta()
    var writable = C::rf_file_map_writable()
    var user = buf.offset(bytes: ByteSize(bytes: delta)).recast_as[T]()
    return FileStorage[T](
        buf: user,
        byte_len: user_len - user_len % esize,
        map_delta: delta,
        writable: writable,
        is_closed: false,
    )

# ============================================================================
# FileManager — listing directories
# ============================================================================

### The direct entries of the directory as bare names (`.` and `..` left out), in the platform's order. Use
### `Directory.file(name:)` / `go_to(name:)` to turn one into a path.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The entry names.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the directory doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it cannot be listed.
failable common routine FileManager.list(at: Directory) -> List[Text]
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_list_dir(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    var len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    if ptr.is_none()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot list '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    if len == 0
        return List[Text]()
    return path_text(ptr: ptr, len: len).split(separator: '\n')

### The direct entries of the directory WITH their kind and modification time, in one directory read.
### Prefer this over `list` + `is_directory` + `metadata` when walking a tree: on Windows the kind and
### mtime come from the directory enumeration, so no per-entry `stat` is needed (POSIX falls back to one
### stat per entry for the mtime). `.` and `..` are left out.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The entries.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the directory doesn't exist, or the crashable for why it cannot be listed.
failable common routine FileManager.entries(at: Directory) -> List[DirEntry]
    var cstr = at.at.to[CStr]()
    var ptr = C::rf_fs_list_dir_typed(path: cstr, len: cstr.count().to[S32]())
    var len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    if ptr.is_none()
        fail_io(action: f"Cannot list '{at.at}'", path: at.at)
    var result = List[DirEntry]()
    if len == 0
        return result
    each line in path_text(ptr: ptr, len: len).split(separator: '\n')
        # Each line is `<kind><mtime> <name>`: 'd'/'f', then decimal Unix seconds, one
        # space, then the raw name (which may itself contain spaces).
        var is_dir = line[0] == 'd'
        var space = line.find(letter: ' ')
        var mtime: S64 = 0
        each k in 1 til space
            mtime = mtime * 10 + line[k].digit_value().to[S64]()
        var name = line[space + 1 til ^0]
        result.add_last(
            value: DirEntry(
                name: name,
                is_directory: is_dir,
                modified: Moment.from_timestamp(seconds: mtime),
            ),
        )
    return result

### Every path under the directory, at any depth, a directory before its contents. Symbolic links are not
### followed.
### :param at: The directory.
### :returns: The full paths.
### :throws: {NotFoundError} when the directory doesn't exist, or the crashable for why a directory in the tree
###     cannot be listed.
failable common routine FileManager.walk(at: Directory) -> List[Text]
    var result = List[Text]()
    each entry in FileManager.entries(at: at)
        result.add_last(value: join_path(a: at.at, b: entry.name))
        if entry.is_directory
            each descendant in FileManager.walk(at: at.go_to(name: entry.name))
                result.add_last(value: descendant)
    return result

# ============================================================================
# Sessions and storages: their own routines
# ============================================================================

### The number of `T` cells in the storage.
### :returns: The resulting `U64` value.
@readonly
routine FileStorage[T].count() -> U64
    return me.byte_len // T.data_size().byte_size()

### Whether the storage was opened for writing (the file could be written).
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
@readonly
routine FileStorage[T].is_writable() -> Bool
    return me.writable

### The `T` in cell `index`.
### :param index: The zero-based cell.
### :returns: The value in that cell.
### :throws: {IndexOutOfBoundsError} if `index >= count()`, {InvalidValueError} when the cell doesn't hold a valid
###     `T` (a `Char` cell that isn't a Unicode scalar).
@readonly
failable routine FileStorage[T].getitem(index: U64) -> T
    var n = me.count()
    if index >= n
        throw IndexOutOfBoundsError(index: index, count: n)
    danger
        var value = me.buf.stride(count: index.to[S64]()).peek()
        if value.is_valid_cell()
            return value
    throw InvalidValueError(input: f"cell {index} of the storage", target: "a Unicode scalar (Char)")

### Writes `value` into cell `index`. The write lands in the file (flushed lazily, on `flush()`, or when the storage
### ends). A storage cannot grow its file: writing past the end is an error.
### :param index: The zero-based cell.
### :param value: The value to store there.
### :throws: {IndexOutOfBoundsError} if `index >= count()`, {NotEnoughPermissionError} when the storage was opened
###     for reading only, {InvalidValueError} when `value` isn't a valid `T` for a cell.
failable routine FileStorage[T].setitem(index: U64, value: T)
    var n = me.count()
    if index >= n
        throw IndexOutOfBoundsError(index: index, count: n)
    if not me.writable
        throw NotEnoughPermissionError(description: f"Cannot write cell {index}: this storage was opened for reading only, since its file can't be written")
    if not value.is_valid_cell()
        throw InvalidValueError(input: f"the value for cell {index}", target: "a Unicode scalar (Char)")
    danger
        me.buf.stride(count: index.to[S64]()).poke(value: value)
    return

### Forces pending writes back to the file now (a durability checkpoint). Writes are otherwise flushed lazily by
### the OS, and always when the storage ends.
routine FileStorage[T].flush()
    if me.is_closed or not me.writable
        return
    danger
        var base = me.buf.recast_as[Byte]().stride(count: 0 -% me.map_delta.to[S64]())
        C::rf_file_map_flush(base, me.byte_len +% me.map_delta)
    return

### Flushes pending writes and releases the mapping, from the true mapping base (`buf` minus `map_delta`) for the
### whole mapped length. Runs once: the storage's end calls it, and later calls do nothing.
routine FileStorage[T].close()
    if me.is_closed
        return
    me.flush()
    danger
        var base = me.buf.recast_as[Byte]().stride(count: 0 -% me.map_delta.to[S64]())
        C::rf_file_unmap(base, me.byte_len +% me.map_delta)
    me.is_closed = true
    return

### Releases the mapping when the storage ends at the end of its scope.
@override
routine FileStorage[T].destruct()
    me.close()
    return

### Loads up to `max` bytes from the current position. Returns fewer when end-of-file is reached first,
### an empty `Bytes` signals EOF.
### :param max: The maximum byte count to load.
### :returns: The bytes loaded.
routine FileSource.load(max: U64) -> Bytes
    var ptr = C::rf_file_read_bytes(me.fd, max)
    var len = C::rf_get_result_len()
    return Bytes(ptr: ptr, count: len)

### Closes the descriptor. Idempotent — `close()`, scope-exit `exit`, and teardown `destruct` never
### double-close.
routine FileSource.close()
    if me.is_closed
        return
    C::rf_file_close(me.fd)
    me.is_closed = true
    return

### Pass-through entry: the descriptor was already acquired by `FileManager.open_read`.
### :returns: This `FileSource`.
failable routine FileSource.enter() -> FileSource
    return me

### Closes the descriptor when leaving a `using` scope.
routine FileSource.exit()
    me.close()
    return

### Closes the descriptor at scope exit (RAII). Idempotent with `exit`/`close()` via the `is_closed`
### guard, so no double-close.
@override
routine FileSource.destruct()
    me.close()
    return

### Stores `data` at the current position, returning the number of bytes accepted.
### :param data: The bytes to store.
### :returns: The number of bytes accepted.
routine FileSink.store(data: Bytes) -> U64
    danger
        return C::rf_file_write(me.fd, data.raw_buffer(), data.count())

### Flushes buffered output to the underlying file.
routine FileSink.flush()
    C::rf_file_flush(me.fd)
    return

### Closes the descriptor. Idempotent — `close()`, scope-exit `exit`, and teardown `destruct` never
### double-close.
routine FileSink.close()
    if me.is_closed
        return
    C::rf_file_close(me.fd)
    me.is_closed = true
    return

### Pass-through entry: the descriptor was already acquired by `FileManager.open_write`/`open_append`.
### :returns: This `FileSink`.
failable routine FileSink.enter() -> FileSink
    return me

### Flushes and closes the descriptor when leaving a `using` scope.
routine FileSink.exit()
    me.flush()
    me.close()
    return

### Closes the descriptor at scope exit (RAII). Idempotent with `exit`/`close()` via the `is_closed`
### guard, so no double-close.
@override
routine FileSink.destruct()
    me.close()
    return
